= Corpus evidence =

This page records the empirical corpus tests used by the [[BITwiki:V1 to V2 maturation|V1 → V2 maturation]] architecture.

The governing question is not “What ontology can we imagine?” It is:

<blockquote>'''What distinctions does the actual corpus require us to represent?'''</blockquote>

The machine-readable evidence is generated during pull-request validation:

* <code>v2-validation.json</code> — structural V2 validation;
* <code>v2-corpus-inventory.json</code> — every deployable V2 MediaWiki page and its objective structural/semantic signals;
* <code>v1-classification-usage.json</code> — all 162 archived V1 Main pages with API-recorded categories/templates, revision identity, subpage state, Infobox type source parameter, classification source syntax, and Topic signal;
* <code>v1-page-role-matrix.json</code> — all 162 V1 Main pages classified by migration role before ontology.

These outputs are evidence artifacts, not a substitute for human interpretation.

== V2 Main-namespace reality ==

At the first complete inventory pass, the V2 Main namespace contained '''49 pages''':

{| class="wikitable"
! Role !! Count
|-
| V1 compatibility or other redirects || 36
|-
| Explicit <code>Knowledge object</code> pages || 12
|-
| Main Page || 1
|-
| Other non-redirect Main pages || 0
|}

The twelve explicit knowledge objects are the twelve domain exemplars. Their explicit identity distribution is:

* Entity type: <code>Concept</code> — 12 / 12;
* Epistemic status: <code>Established</code> — 12 / 12;
* Domain: one exemplar in each current domain — Systems science, Science, Biology, Mathematics, Philosophy, Technology, Electronics, Energy, Engineering, Chemistry, Physics, and Medicine.

'''V2 is therefore a strong architectural/semantic scaffold whose canonical subject corpus is still deliberately small.'''

This does not prove that every future BITwiki object is a Concept. It proves only that the present exemplar surface exercises the Concept path and cannot by itself finalize the broader type/domain vocabularies.

